 ## Overview
-Back-face culling for ray casting is an optimization technique that determines the entry and exit points of a ray into a volume by rendering the back faces of the bounding cube to a texture, thereby avoiding the need to compute ray-cube intersections analytically [S1, unofficial]. It is a two-pass rendering technique: the first pass renders the backface of the bounding box to a framebuffer to record entry depths, and the second pass performs raycasting using those points to drive ray marching, ensuring sampling begins inside the volume and terminates at the exit point [S2, S3, unofficial]. This method sets itself apart from analytical intersection methods by leveraging the graphics pipeline's rasterization and depth testing to implicitly solve for ray bounds, and from 3D texture-based entry/exit lookups by using a 2D texture derived from the backface projection [S2, unofficial].
