 ## Background
-The method originated as an optimization in GPU-based volume rendering to replace CPU-side or complex shader-side ray-cube intersection calculations with hardware-accelerated rasterization and depth testing. It leverages the fact that rendering the back faces of a bounding box to a depth buffer (with `GEQUAL` depth function) effectively records the entry depth of rays into the volume. The technique is documented in various unofficial repositories and tutorials focusing on WebGL and GLSL volume rendering [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It is a variant of hierarchical traversal methods that use coarse meshes, specifically simplifying the entry/exit determination for a cubic bounding box [987-coarse-mesh-traversal-with-dual-ray-culling.md].
